//! The String module: byte-wise operations on a `string`, none of which allocate.
//!
//! Written in Jairs, like `Basic`, because PLAN.md decision #5 puts the standard library in the language
//! rather than in the compiler. This is the second module the project has, and the first that is not
//! `Basic` — so it is also the first real exercise of importing two modules at once (ADR-0014's flat merge).
//!
//! # Why this module exists, and why it is W7's first
//!
//! ADR-0099 §4 refused `==` on two strings: a `string` is `{data: *u8, count: s64}` (ADR-0004), so "the same
//! storage" and "the same contents" are both plausible readings, and picking one silently would make the
//! other a bug that looks like working code. That refusal's own reasoning named the fix — comparing contents
//! needs a byte loop — and a byte loop is a *library*'s job, not an operator's. So the previous wave named
//! this module as the answer to a refusal it raised, which is the clearest possible reason to write it first.
//!
//! An `==` that looped would also be the only implicitly-looping operator in the language, which is a much
//! larger thing to introduce than a procedure called `equal`.
//!
//! # Why nothing here allocates
//!
//! `concat`, `substring`, `to_upper` and `split` are all deliberately absent. Each needs somewhere to put its
//! result, which means an allocator argument and a decision about who frees it. `context.allocator` exists
//! (ADR-0057) and so does temporary storage (ADR-0065), so the mechanism is not missing — but *choosing*
//! between "always the context allocator", "an explicit parameter" and "always temporary" has real
//! consequences for every caller, and it deserves its own decision rather than being settled in passing by
//! whichever one this file happened to use.
//!
//! A non-allocating module is a complete and useful thing on its own. Shipping it first means the allocation
//! decision gets made with a working baseline to compare against.
//!
//! # Why `byte_at` exists at all
//!
//! `s.data[i]` **does not compile**: `data` is a `*u8` and a pointer is not indexable (E0234). Reading a byte
//! therefore takes `(s.data + i).*` plus a cast to `s64`, which is pointer arithmetic in the middle of
//! otherwise ordinary code. `byte_at` is that expression with a name, and it is honest about being a
//! workaround: when pointer indexing arrives, this becomes a one-line wrapper rather than the only way.
//!
//! # What is checked, and where
//!
//! `tests/corpus/valid/084-string.jr` exercises every procedure here and exits on a value that depends on all
//! of them, so both engines must agree on each answer. That matters more than usual for this module: a
//! comparison routine that returned a constant would pass any test that only asked "did it run".

/// The byte at `index`, or `-1` when the index is out of range.
///
/// **`-1` rather than a trap**, unlike an out-of-range array index (ADR-0003): an array's bound is known to
/// the compiler and indexing past it is a *mistake*, while scanning a string until the bytes run out is an
/// ordinary way to write a loop. A caller who wants the strict reading can compare against `count` first.
///
/// The cast to `s64` is not a convenience: a `u8` cannot be compared to an integer literal without one
/// (ADR-0016 §1 gives a literal its type from context, and there is no implicit widening — ADR-0015), so
/// returning `u8` would push a cast into every caller.
byte_at :: (s: string, index: s64) -> s64 {
    if index < 0 {
        return -1;
    }
    if index >= s.count {
        return -1;
    }
    p := s.data + index;
    return cast(s64, p.*);
}

/// Whether `s` has no bytes.
///
/// One line, and worth a name: `is_empty(s)` says what it means at a call site where `s.count == 0` says how
/// it is measured.
is_empty :: (s: string) -> bool {
    return s.count == 0;
}

/// Whether `a` and `b` have the same bytes.
///
/// **The reason this module exists.** E0278's help says "compare `.count`, or compare fields one at a time";
/// this is the real answer it was pointing at.
///
/// Lengths are compared first, which is not merely an optimisation: it is what makes the loop below safe to
/// write against one bound instead of two.
equal :: (a: string, b: string) -> bool {
    if a.count != b.count {
        return false;
    }
    i := 0;
    while i < a.count {
        if byte_at(a, i) != byte_at(b, i) {
            return false;
        }
        i = i + 1;
    }
    return true;
}

/// Negative when `a` sorts before `b`, zero when they are equal, positive when after.
///
/// Byte order, not locale or case: a `string` is bytes (ADR-0004), and anything else would need a notion of
/// encoding this language does not have. A shorter string that is a prefix of a longer one sorts first, which
/// is the ordinary lexicographic rule.
///
/// The **sign** is what is specified, not the magnitude — a caller must not read the difference of two bytes
/// out of it, because that would pin an implementation detail no other comparison routine promises.
compare :: (a: string, b: string) -> s64 {
    i := 0;
    while i < a.count {
        if i >= b.count {
            // `a` is longer and matched so far, so `b` is a prefix of `a`.
            return 1;
        }
        x := byte_at(a, i);
        y := byte_at(b, i);
        if x < y {
            return -1;
        }
        if x > y {
            return 1;
        }
        i = i + 1;
    }
    if b.count > a.count {
        return -1;
    }
    return 0;
}

/// Whether `s` begins with `prefix`.
///
/// An empty prefix is `true`, which is the reading every other language takes and the one that makes
/// `starts_with(s, "")` composable rather than a special case a caller has to guard.
starts_with :: (s: string, prefix: string) -> bool {
    if prefix.count > s.count {
        return false;
    }
    i := 0;
    while i < prefix.count {
        if byte_at(s, i) != byte_at(prefix, i) {
            return false;
        }
        i = i + 1;
    }
    return true;
}

/// Whether `s` ends with `suffix`.
///
/// The offset is computed once rather than being folded into the index expression, because the interesting
/// thing about this procedure is that it reads `s` from a *different* starting point than `starts_with`, and
/// naming the offset is what makes that visible.
ends_with :: (s: string, suffix: string) -> bool {
    if suffix.count > s.count {
        return false;
    }
    offset := s.count - suffix.count;
    i := 0;
    while i < suffix.count {
        if byte_at(s, offset + i) != byte_at(suffix, i) {
            return false;
        }
        i = i + 1;
    }
    return true;
}

/// The index of the first occurrence of `needle` in `haystack`, or `-1`.
///
/// **`-1` rather than a second return value**, even though Jairs has multiple returns (ADR-0008): a caller of
/// `find` almost always feeds the result straight into a comparison, and `if find(h, n) >= 0` reads better than
/// naming an index it is about to discard. The error model earns its keep where the *value* is meaningless on
/// failure; here the sentinel is outside the domain of valid indices, so it cannot be mistaken for one.
///
/// An empty needle is found at 0, which follows from "every string starts with the empty string".
find :: (haystack: string, needle: string) -> s64 {
    if needle.count > haystack.count {
        return -1;
    }
    // The last start position worth trying: past it the needle cannot fit.
    last := haystack.count - needle.count;
    start := 0;
    while start <= last {
        i := 0;
        matched := true;
        while i < needle.count {
            if byte_at(haystack, start + i) != byte_at(needle, i) {
                matched = false;
                // The inner loop stops on the first mismatch rather than running to the end: `while` has no
                // `break` out of a nested loop without a label (ADR-0049), and setting the bound is clearer
                // here than labelling.
                i = needle.count;
            } else {
                i = i + 1;
            }
        }
        if matched {
            return start;
        }
        start = start + 1;
    }
    return -1;
}

/// Whether `needle` occurs anywhere in `haystack`.
///
/// Named separately from `find` because a predicate at a call site should not have to mention an index it does
/// not use — the same argument `is_empty` makes against `count == 0`.
contains :: (haystack: string, needle: string) -> bool {
    return find(haystack, needle) >= 0;
}
